(1180819, 146047, 33560, '<td valign="top">There was a time when I tried using photoshop effects to improve a photo that had problems.  Then I decided that it was usually a bad idea.  In this case I think more could have been gained by fixing the problem with the blinds - the big black spaces that occur beneath their lower edges - and moving that shiny wire thingy .  The edge of the table on which the elbow rests is a bit of a problem - more precisely the fact that the lower edge is in the photo.  The pose does not strike me as natural or photogenic and it is a hard pose to crop well. <br/> <br/></td>', 'There was a time when I tried using photoshop effects to improve a photo that had problems.  Then I decided that it was usually a bad idea.  In this case I think more could have been gained by fixing the problem with the blinds - the big black spaces that occur beneath their lower edges - and moving that shiny wire thingy .  The edge of the table on which the elbow rests is a bit of a problem - more precisely the fact that the lower edge is in the photo.  The pose does not strike me as natural or photogenic and it is a hard pose to crop well.  ', '2005-02-18 18:41:14.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180843, 145948, 33560, '<td valign="top">I am almost always ruthless about distractions in a photo.  But here the photo is about a person and his relationship to music.  I find the soft earthtones of the shot to be remarkably soothing and they harmonize nicely.   I also like the rhythms established by the book bindings and the staves.  It is always unexpected to have a subject''s face totally in the shadow, and it is always a big risk.  Here it is carried off with aplomb.  Usually I find washed out and black areas wasted, but here the areas echo each other in shape, and the black and white give high and low notes to the composition.  In a field of very predictable photos, this one is unexpected: it''s a gem.</td>', 'I am almost always ruthless about distractions in a photo.  But here the photo is about a person and his relationship to music.  I find the soft earthtones of the shot to be remarkably soothing and they harmonize nicely.   I also like the rhythms established by the book bindings and the staves.  It is always unexpected to have a subject''s face totally in the shadow, and it is always a big risk.  Here it is carried off with aplomb.  Usually I find washed out and black areas wasted, but here the areas echo each other in shape, and the black and white give high and low notes to the composition.  In a field of very predictable photos, this one is unexpected: it''s a gem.', '2005-02-18 18:46:23.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180868, 145611, 33560, '<td valign="top">This one definitely had the most striking thumbnail.  But it''s purposeful slight blurriness  - especially  of the eyes which would make the photo extraordinary - is its ruin.  When eyes are lit so extraordinarily, we''d like at least one to be in focus.  It''s like going to an expensive French restaurant where everyone is eating the best cuisine the world has to offer and being served a bowl of Instant Oatmeal.</td>', 'This one definitely had the most striking thumbnail.  But it''s purposeful slight blurriness  - especially  of the eyes which would make the photo extraordinary - is its ruin.  When eyes are lit so extraordinarily, we''d like at least one to be in focus.  It''s like going to an expensive French restaurant where everyone is eating the best cuisine the world has to offer and being served a bowl of Instant Oatmeal.', '2005-02-18 18:53:50.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180869, 146459, 280, '<td valign="top">Funky photo, but I just wish there was more focus on the eyes. 7</td>', 'Funky photo, but I just wish there was more focus on the eyes. 7', '2005-02-18 18:54:04.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180873, 146828, 23394, '<td valign="top">while i appreciate what you''ve attempted here, i am left feeling that i am seeing more "red" than "you, mad".  i know this wasn''t easy, but i can''t help but feel that the image would be stronger if a little more of your face, especially your eyes, had come through.  good luck!</td>', 'while i appreciate what you''ve attempted here, i am left feeling that i am seeing more "red" than "you, mad".  i know this wasn''t easy, but i can''t help but feel that the image would be stronger if a little more of your face, especially your eyes, had come through.  good luck!', '2005-02-18 18:55:09.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180890, 145305, 23394, '<td valign="top">i don''t know anything about portraiture, so i''m using your image to try to figure out what i think i would or wouldn''t do in this situation, as well as what i notice and get out of your image.<br/> <br/>i know this is a hard shot to take, and i think you''ve made a nice effort.  the lighting is nice, but the focus is a bit soft.  that would be ok, but the angle of the head leaves me feeling that you would rather be on the other side of the lens.  good luck!</td>', 'i don''t know anything about portraiture, so i''m using your image to try to figure out what i think i would or wouldn''t do in this situation, as well as what i notice and get out of your image. i know this is a hard shot to take, and i think you''ve made a nice effort.  the lighting is nice, but the focus is a bit soft.  that would be ok, but the angle of the head leaves me feeling that you would rather be on the other side of the lens.  good luck!', '2005-02-18 19:02:01.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180892, 145678, 23394, '<td valign="top">i don''t know anything about portraiture, so i''m using your image to try to figure out what i think i would or wouldn''t do in this situation, as well as what i notice and get out of your image.<br/> <br/>killer job with the lighting!  that piercing gaze is just INTENSE.  if the art of portraiture is capturing the essence of the individual, i would have to say you''ve succeeded.  the lighting, focus, composition, colors, and expression all come together to make a very powerful image.  great work.  good luck!</td>', 'i don''t know anything about portraiture, so i''m using your image to try to figure out what i think i would or wouldn''t do in this situation, as well as what i notice and get out of your image. killer job with the lighting!  that piercing gaze is just INTENSE.  if the art of portraiture is capturing the essence of the individual, i would have to say you''ve succeeded.  the lighting, focus, composition, colors, and expression all come together to make a very powerful image.  great work.  good luck!', '2005-02-18 19:04:48.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180893, 146516, 3032, '<td valign="top">Interesting how the shadows line up with the structure that creates the shadows. This feels very scandinavian, all that space, the big windows... The positioning is a bit confusing, but with the shadows it works well.</td>', 'Interesting how the shadows line up with the structure that creates the shadows. This feels very scandinavian, all that space, the big windows... The positioning is a bit confusing, but with the shadows it works well.', '2005-02-18 19:05:05.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180897, 146420, 33560, '<td valign="top">Yes, there is a B&amp;W film I get this look with .. but never mind.  I imagine one could do the same thing with levels and curves  - gotta try.  I''ve now been at this for two or three hours and  even though B&amp;W is overused this is the first photo I''ve seen in thie competition that has the visceral feel of a B&amp;W print from film.  It''s a nice look  (gotta check out the vintage section of this site, I guess) I like the composition, lighting, and richness of texture in hair, iron, wood, plaster, and chickenwire.  Very nice.</td>', 'Yes, there is a B&W film I get this look with .. but never mind.  I imagine one could do the same thing with levels and curves  - gotta try.  I''ve now been at this for two or three hours and  even though B&W is overused this is the first photo I''ve seen in thie competition that has the visceral feel of a B&W print from film.  It''s a nice look  (gotta check out the vintage section of this site, I guess) I like the composition, lighting, and richness of texture in hair, iron, wood, plaster, and chickenwire.  Very nice.', '2005-02-18 19:06:20.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180900, 145438, 33560, '<td valign="top">Nice bokeh, but the flat lighting... falls flat.</td>', 'Nice bokeh, but the flat lighting... falls flat.', '2005-02-18 19:07:19.000000', NULL, 1),
(1180901, 145749, 23394, '<td valign="top">i don''t know anything about portraiture, so i''m using your image to try to figure out what i think i would or wouldn''t do in this situation, as well as what i notice and get out of your image.<br/> <br/>what an excellent capture.  you look so at home with this, it''s hard to believe that it is a self-portrait.  i wish i could feel as comfortable in front of a lens as how you come across.  the lighting is perfect.  such a lovely range of tones and details.  i really hope this does well for you!  thanks for sharing!</td>', 'i don''t know anything about portraiture, so i''m using your image to try to figure out what i think i would or wouldn''t do in this situation, as well as what i notice and get out of your image. what an excellent capture.  you look so at home with this, it''s hard to believe that it is a self-portrait.  i wish i could feel as comfortable in front of a lens as how you come across.  the lighting is perfect.  such a lovely range of tones and details.  i really hope this does well for you!  thanks for sharing!', '2005-02-18 19:07:25.000000', NULL, 1),
