(1190621, 145879, 19010, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the critique club!!<br/> <br/>Initial impact: Woa thats dark.. hmm gangsta..ok it fits.<br/> <br/>Does it meet the challenge: Well so long as thats realy you in the pfoto I would have to say yes.<br/> <br/>Focus: Very Nice and crisp I don''t think you could have nailed it better.<br/> <br/>Color: well theres not much color in this shot. I don''t feel that alot of colors would have been appropriate for this shot either. I tend to think however that I would have liked this shot a little better if your eyes had been a tiny bit brighter. The are a strong feature in this shot and that they were underplayed.<br/> <br/>Lighting: I tend to disagree with the others however on this aspect. I think that the lighting was just right. I see gangsta''s as being dark and shadowy. I feel tht the stong shadows in this case add to the feeling of danger. <br/> <br/>Composition: The layout you used works very well. I think however that due to the fact that your only realy showing the one eye. That a bit of an agle from the side might have looked better. I think that this might have given you a more candid appearance.<br/> <br/>Overall: Very strong image. I dislike the gangsta''s that I have met in person and don''t usualy care for there nature. I feel that you may have lost a very slight amount of points due to this factor. Despite my dislike for the subject matter, I still would not be able to score this photo any way but high.  Powerfull shot Keep the great work coming.<br/> <br/>Tristalisk</td>', 'Greetings from the critique club!! Initial impact: Woa thats dark.. hmm gangsta..ok it fits. Does it meet the challenge: Well so long as thats realy you in the pfoto I would have to say yes. Focus: Very Nice and crisp I don''t think you could have nailed it better. Color: well theres not much color in this shot. I don''t feel that alot of colors would have been appropriate for this shot either. I tend to think however that I would have liked this shot a little better if your eyes had been a tiny bit brighter. The are a strong feature in this shot and that they were underplayed. Lighting: I tend to disagree with the others however on this aspect. I think that the lighting was just right. I see gangsta''s as being dark and shadowy. I feel tht the stong shadows in this case add to the feeling of danger.  Composition: The layout you used works very well. I think however that due to the fact that your only realy showing the one eye. That a bit of an agle from the side might have looked better. I think that this might have given you a more candid appearance. Overall: Very strong image. I dislike the gangsta''s that I have met in person and don''t usualy care for there nature. I feel that you may have lost a very slight amount of points due to this factor. Despite my dislike for the subject matter, I still would not be able to score this photo any way but high.  Powerfull shot Keep the great work coming. Tristalisk', '2005-02-23 11:42:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(1190973, 146885, 15407, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Beryl:</b></div><hr/><i> AHKH-truh - does that define the happiness in your expression? </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Yes it does in a way... The snapshot I took illustrates the elation I felt from having just returned home on my BMW "Adventure" ridding it for first time since my camera, motorcycle and I were run over by a hit &amp; run driver in San Francisco (August 7, 2004). Upon my arrival home I also found that my camera was repaired and waiting for me on the doorstep. The smile was genuine &amp; the adventure has been a good one!  EACHTRA is the Irish word for "Adventure"<br/></td>', 'Originally posted by Beryl: AHKH-truh - does that define the happiness in your expression?  Yes it does in a way... The snapshot I took illustrates the elation I felt from having just returned home on my BMW "Adventure" ridding it for first time since my camera, motorcycle and I were run over by a hit & run driver in San Francisco (August 7, 2004). Upon my arrival home I also found that my camera was repaired and waiting for me on the doorstep. The smile was genuine & the adventure has been a good one!  EACHTRA is the Irish word for "Adventure"', '2005-02-23 14:12:24.000000', NULL, 0),
(1191198, 146577, 16759, '<td valign="top">Vá þú ert falleg og tekur flottar myndir, já sagði einhver draumakonan????<br/>en flott mynd þú ætlar bara ekki að skila neinu slæmu frá þér það er á hreinu...<br/>Hörður Ásbjörnsson</td>', 'Vá þú ert falleg og tekur flottar myndir, já sagði einhver draumakonan????en flott mynd þú ætlar bara ekki að skila neinu slæmu frá þér það er á hreinu...Hörður Ásbjörnsson', '2005-02-23 15:50:56.000000', NULL, 0),
(1191213, 146162, 19010, '<td valign="top">Well bear music I''ll squeek on in here and admit to being one of the 2''s. If this had been shot for a different challenge I''ll use the brokeh challenge for an example. I feel my score probably would have been around a 6 or 7. From a technical aspect its a decent photo. I think that the end of the twig stopping abrubtly in the photo is a bit distracing. It leaves me with an unfinished feel. I do however enjoy the shallow DOF, and the mood that the shot portays. I''f this had been a shot of your dog,wife, or kids. I probably would have agreed it fit the challenge. I thought about this shot for a bit before I scored it. I didn''t feel that this represented you. Your kids might be part of you, your wife may have made you the way you are, but I was unable to see this lovely water drop hanging from a thorn as a photo of who someone is. I don''t intend to be defending myself. I Just felt that someone who felt this was a low scoring shot should explain themself. So that perhaps you could better understand why the photo scored as it did. Well I hope I was of some help to you. May you have better luck in the Future.<br/> <br/>Tristalisk</td>', 'Well bear music I''ll squeek on in here and admit to being one of the 2''s. If this had been shot for a different challenge I''ll use the brokeh challenge for an example. I feel my score probably would have been around a 6 or 7. From a technical aspect its a decent photo. I think that the end of the twig stopping abrubtly in the photo is a bit distracing. It leaves me with an unfinished feel. I do however enjoy the shallow DOF, and the mood that the shot portays. I''f this had been a shot of your dog,wife, or kids. I probably would have agreed it fit the challenge. I thought about this shot for a bit before I scored it. I didn''t feel that this represented you. Your kids might be part of you, your wife may have made you the way you are, but I was unable to see this lovely water drop hanging from a thorn as a photo of who someone is. I don''t intend to be defending myself. I Just felt that someone who felt this was a low scoring shot should explain themself. So that perhaps you could better understand why the photo scored as it did. Well I hope I was of some help to you. May you have better luck in the Future. Tristalisk', '2005-02-23 15:58:57.000000', NULL, 0),
(1191447, 146592, 8860, '<td valign="top"><b>:: Hello from the Critique Club ::</b> <br/>............................................................<br/> <br/>Well, I looked at this and immediately had a flashback to an another era - like a surrealist from the 40s, perhaps even, dare I say, a Bill Brandt. For all the obvious (and deliberate) flaws and bucked conventions, this shot proves to be very memorable. <br/> <br/>The pose for me shows a side of you that is slightly anarchic and resistant to the regular - which would not haved worked if the subject were to conform to the usual rules of cropping and composition. As such it has an indefinable X-factor which stands it apart from the crowd.<br/> <br/>And more importantly, I like it!<br/> <br/>Cheers<br/>Jon</td>', ':: Hello from the Critique Club :: ............................................................ Well, I looked at this and immediately had a flashback to an another era - like a surrealist from the 40s, perhaps even, dare I say, a Bill Brandt. For all the obvious (and deliberate) flaws and bucked conventions, this shot proves to be very memorable.  The pose for me shows a side of you that is slightly anarchic and resistant to the regular - which would not haved worked if the subject were to conform to the usual rules of cropping and composition. As such it has an indefinable X-factor which stands it apart from the crowd. And more importantly, I like it! CheersJon', '2005-02-23 17:53:40.000000', NULL, 0),
(1191476, 146221, 12611, '<td valign="top">This is well done, expressive, good composition adn contrast. Very good at displaying a touch of innocence with a feel of experience, whatever that means. good job.</td>', 'This is well done, expressive, good composition adn contrast. Very good at displaying a touch of innocence with a feel of experience, whatever that means. good job.', '2005-02-23 18:07:21.000000', NULL, 0),
